// +gobra

package router

import "net"

// Permission introspection for the types that occur in the dataplane state.
//
// Ideally, the functions below would live in
// `github.com/scionproto/scion/verification/utils/permsintrospect`, together with
// all other permission introspection functions. That is not possible: their
// signatures mention `net.UDPAddr`, so `permsintrospect` would have to import the
// `net` stubs. `net` is a dependency of almost every package here, this one
// included, and it must stay free to introspect permissions itself, so the two
// packages would then import each other. Gobra rejects cyclical imports.
//
// The convention we follow is therefore: `permsintrospect` imports nothing and
// only declares introspection functions for built-in types, while an
// introspection function whose signature mentions a type or a predicate declared
// in some other package Q lives in Q, in a file named `perm-introspect.gobra`.
// The map type below is specific to the dataplane, so this package, and not the
// `net` stubs, is its natural home.
//
// See the documentation of the `permsintrospect` package for why these functions
// are axioms and what they may be assumed to provide.

// Names the permission held to a map of next hops. The nil case is admitted
// because the dataplane consistently guards the permission to its maps by a nil
// check (cf. `internalNextHopsInv`), which lets clients introspect without
// having to case-split first. For a nil map, the returned amount is positive but
// otherwise meaningless.
ghost
requires m != nil ==> acc(m, _)
ensures  0 < p
ensures  m != nil ==> acc(m, p)
decreases
func PermIntrospectAddrsMap(m map[uint16]*net.UDPAddr) (p perm)
